import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var n={nuclearFission:{theory:{concept:"[[Nuclear fission is the splitting of a heavy nucleus, such as uranium-235, into two lighter nuclei, which releases a large amount of energy and 2 or 3 neutrons.]] A uranium-235 nucleus splits after it absorbs a slow neutron. The products together are a little lighter than the nucleus and the neutron before the fission, and the missing mass becomes energy, E = \u0394m\xB7c\xB2 \u2014 about 200 MeV per fission, millions of times more than in any chemical reaction. The freed neutrons can split further nuclei: this is a chain reaction. If on average more than one of them causes a new fission (k > 1), the reaction grows; if fewer (k < 1), it dies out. A reactor keeps k at exactly 1."},section:{discovery:{title:"An unexpected discovery",text:`In December 1938 Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann bombarded uranium with neutrons in Berlin and, to their surprise, found barium \u2014 an element about half as heavy. A few weeks later Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch explained it: the nucleus had split in two. Frisch borrowed the word "fission" from biology, where cells divide. Four years later, on 2 December 1942, Enrico Fermi's team in Chicago ran the first self-sustaining chain reaction, in a pile of natural uranium and graphite.`},splitting:{title:"How a nucleus splits",text:"A large nucleus is like a wobbly drop of liquid. The strong nuclear force holds it together, while the electric repulsion of its 92 protons pushes it apart. When U-235 absorbs a slow neutron, the new nucleus U-236 starts to vibrate, stretches into a dumbbell and the electric repulsion tears it in two \u2014 for example into barium-141 and krypton-92. The two fragments fly apart very fast (the red dots), and 2 or 3 neutrons are set free. Sometimes (in about 1 case in 7) U-236 does not split and just sends out gamma radiation: the grey nuclei in the simulation."},energy:{title:"Where the energy comes from",text:"Add up the masses after a fission and you find about 0.1 % less than before. This mass defect turns into energy according to Einstein's E = \u0394m\xB7c\xB2: about 200 MeV per nucleus, mostly as the motion of the fragments, which heats the fuel. Burning one carbon atom gives only about 4 eV, so one fission gives about 50 million times more. One gram of U-235 releases about 8\xB710\xB9\u2070 J \u2014 as much as burning nearly 3 tonnes of coal."},chain:{title:"The chain reaction and the factor k",text:"Follow one neutron until it ends: it causes a fission, is absorbed somewhere else, or escapes. [[The multiplication factor k is the average number of new neutrons that one neutron leads to.]] After g generations there are N\u2080\xB7k^g neutrons. With k = 1.5, ten generations turn 1 neutron into about 58; with k = 0.8 they shrink to about 0.1 \u2014 the chain stops. In the simulation k is estimated for the fuel that is left, so you can watch it fall as the fuel burns."},critical:{title:"Critical size",text:"Neutrons born near the edge can escape before they hit anything. The bigger the core, the smaller the share that escapes, because the surface grows more slowly than the volume. At the critical size k = 1 exactly: smaller is subcritical, bigger is supercritical. A reflector around the core sends escaping neutrons back and lowers the critical size. For a bare sphere of pure U-235 metal the critical mass is about 52 kg; in a reactor, with water and enriched uranium, it is different."},enrichment:{title:"Why uranium is enriched",text:"Natural uranium is 99.3 % U-238 and only 0.72 % U-235. Slow neutrons hardly split U-238, but it absorbs some of them and so takes neutrons away from the chain. Raising the share of U-235 (enrichment) makes k larger. Power reactors use 3\u20135 % U-235. With slow neutrons, enrichment above a few per cent helps less and less; an atomic bomb works with fast neutrons and needs about 90 %."},control:{title:"Controlling a reactor",text:"[[Control rods made of boron or cadmium absorb neutrons very strongly.]] Pushing them in lowers k, pulling them out raises it, so operators hold k at exactly 1 and the power stays steady. Water in the core is the moderator: it slows the neutrons down, because slow neutrons split U-235 far more easily. Control is possible only because about 0.65 % of the neutrons come out seconds after the fission (delayed neutrons), which gives the rods time to move \u2014 this simulation does not include them."},reactorBomb:{title:"A reactor, a bomb and safety",text:"In a reactor k = 1: each generation is as large as the last and heat is released steadily for years. In a bomb a supercritical mass is assembled within microseconds and k is much larger than 1, so an enormous number of fissions happens before the material flies apart. Reactors cannot explode like a bomb, but they can overheat: in Chernobyl in 1986 the power rose out of control and the reactor was destroyed. The fission fragments are radioactive, so used fuel has to be stored safely for a very long time."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"Why does a small piece of even 90 % U-235 not explode? (Too many neutrons escape: k < 1.) What does k = 1 mean? (Every generation has the same number of neutrons: steady power.) Why do control rods work? (They absorb neutrons and lower k.) Why does a big chain reaction stop by itself in the simulation? (The U-235 is used up and k falls below 1.) Why are reactors not built from natural uranium and water? (U-238 absorbs too many neutrons.) Try each one."}},formula:{reaction:"One possible fission of uranium-235",massEnergy:"The mass defect \u0394m becomes energy",generations:"Number of neutrons after g generations",k:"Multiplication factor: new neutrons per neutron (\u03BD: neutrons per fission)",energy:"Energy released by N_f fissions",eta:"Neutrons released per neutron absorbed in the uranium",survival:"Chance that a neutron travels a path s without being absorbed"},variable:{nu:"Neutrons released by one fission (2 or 3, on average 2.43)",ng:"Number of neutrons in generation g",massDefect:"Mass defect: how much lighter the products are",light:"Speed of light, 3\xB710\u2078 m/s",sigma:"Cross section: how large a target a nucleus is for a neutron (barn, 10\u207B\xB2\u2078 m\xB2)",bigSigma:"Absorption per unit length of path: number of nuclei per volume \xD7 cross section"},assumption:{twoD:"The core is a flat disc and neutrons fly in straight lines; in reality they bounce many times off the nuclei of the water before they are absorbed.",thermal:"All neutrons are slow (thermal): slowing down in the water is not shown. The probabilities come from the cross sections of slow neutrons (U-235: fission 585 b, absorption 684 b; U-238: absorption 2.68 b). U-238 and the water are not drawn: they absorb like an even background.",drawing:"Each drawn nucleus or neutron stands for very many. Sizes, distances and times are not to scale and the motion is slowed down enormously: a neutron generation in a real reactor lasts about a ten-thousandth of a second.",delayed:"Delayed neutrons are ignored, and the fuel does not heat up or expand. In a real reactor these effects make control possible.",energy:"Every fission releases 200 MeV and 2 or 3 neutrons (on average 2.43). The fragments are shown only for a moment, and their radioactivity is not followed.",estimate:"k is estimated by following 500 test neutrons that start at random U-235 nuclei, so it changes a little from run to run."},param:{fuel:"Amount of fuel (U-235 nuclei)",enrichment:"Enrichment (share of U-235)",rods:"Control rods pushed in",reflector:"Neutron reflector around the core",startNeutrons:"Neutrons at the start",seed:"Experiment number (chance)",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{fuel:"More fuel makes a bigger core, from which neutrons escape less easily.",enrichment:"Natural uranium has 0.72 % U-235; power reactors use 3\u20135 %, bombs about 90 %.",rods:"Rods absorb neutrons. They can be moved while the reactor runs.",reflector:"Sends half of the escaping neutrons back into the core.",startNeutrons:"Free neutrons that start the chain reaction.",seed:"Fission is random: another number gives another run with the same settings."},preset:{small:"Small (40)",medium:"Medium (100)",large:"Large (400)",natural:"Natural 0.72 %",reactor:"Reactor 5 %",research:"Research 20 %",weapons:"Weapons 90 %",rodsOut:"Out",rodsHalf:"Half",rodsIn:"Fully in"},measure:{fissions:"Fissions",neutrons:"Free neutrons now",energy:"Energy released",kNow:"Multiplication factor",kStart:"Multiplication factor at the start",generation:"Neutron generation",fuelUsed:"U-235 used up",maxNeutrons:"Most free neutrons at once",generationK:"Measured k (last full generation)",fate:{fission:"Neutrons that caused a fission",u235:"Captured by U-235 without fission",u238:"Absorbed by U-238",rod:"Absorbed by the control rods",leak:"Escaped from the core"},eta:"Neutrons per neutron absorbed in uranium",energyJoule:"Energy released"},graph:{neutrons:"Free neutrons vs time",fissions:"Fissions vs time",k:"Multiplication factor vs time"},view:{subcritical:"k = {k} < 1: subcritical \u2014 the chain reaction dies out",critical:"k = {k} \u2248 1: critical \u2014 the chain reaction holds steady",supercritical:"k = {k} > 1: supercritical \u2014 the chain reaction grows",stopped:"The chain reaction stopped after {fissions} fissions (k now = {k})",unlucky:"The chain died out by chance after {fissions} fissions, although k = {kStart} > 1. With few neutrons, luck matters: try another experiment number or more starting neutrons.",stillRunning:"Time is up: {fissions} fissions, and the reaction is still running (k = {k})",reflector:"reflector",legend:"large dots: U-235 \xB7 grey: U-236 \xB7 small dots: neutrons \xB7 pairs: fission fragments \xB7 bars: control rods",fragmentsOption:"Fission flashes and fragments"},challenge:{chain:{title:"A chain reaction",description:"Get at least 100 fissions in one run.",hint:"Too many neutrons escape from a small core. Use more fuel."},fizzle:{title:"Too small to work",description:"Use weapons-grade uranium (at least 80 %) and still keep the core subcritical (k at the start below 0.95).",hint:"Enrichment is not enough: with little fuel most neutrons escape."},half:{title:"Burn half of it",description:"Split or use up at least half of the U-235 in one run.",hint:"A strongly supercritical core keeps going until the fuel runs low."},critical:{title:"Exactly critical",description:"Use the control rods to set k = 1.00 (\xB1 0.03).",hint:"Start from a supercritical core and push the rods in slowly while you watch k."},shutdown:{title:"Emergency stop",description:"With at least 300 nuclei of 20 % uranium or more, make k smaller than 0.9.",hint:"Push the control rods deep into the core."},reflector:{title:"A small supercritical core",description:"Make a core of at most 40 nuclei supercritical (k > 1.05).",hint:"A reflector sends escaping neutrons back."},natural:{title:"Fermi's way",description:"With natural uranium (0.72 %), get at least 100 fissions in one run.",hint:"Use the biggest core and a reflector. More starting neutrons, or another experiment number, can help the chain survive its first generations."}}}},s={sim:n};export{s as default,n as sim};
